From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2_codec2: give single-planar encoder input the device's
 buffer length

For a single-planar input format (NV12 rather than NV12M), enqueueInputBuffer()
sets the plane length to allocationSize() of the coded size, i.e. the bytes the
frame occupies (stride x aligned height x 1.5). The device's sizeimage can be
larger: Qualcomm iris rounds the frame size up to a multiple of 4 KiB.
videobuf2 rejects a DMABUF plane whose length is below sizeimage, so QBUF fails
with EINVAL and the component goes to the error state on the first frame. With
the videobuf2 debug output enabled, 120x160 (stride 128) shows "invalid dmabuf
length 30720 for plane 0, minimum length 32768". On iris this hits every size
whose stride is an odd multiple of 128 and whose height, aligned to 32, is an
odd multiple of 32, e.g. 640x480, 800x480, 854x480, 352x288 and 120x160.

Use the device's size as the length, as the multi-planar branch already does,
and keep bytesused at the frame size. The gralloc buffers used as encoder input
are allocated in whole pages and are large enough.

Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 v4l2/V4L2Encoder.cpp | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/v4l2/V4L2Encoder.cpp b/v4l2/V4L2Encoder.cpp
index 97aaa27..38ec2ce 100644
--- a/v4l2/V4L2Encoder.cpp
+++ b/v4l2/V4L2Encoder.cpp
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 #include <v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Encoder.h>
 
 #include <stdint.h>
+#include <algorithm>
 #include <optional>
 #include <vector>
 
@@ -852,7 +853,11 @@ bool V4L2Encoder::enqueueInputBuffer(std::unique_ptr<InputFrame> frame) {
         buffer->setPlaneDataOffset(0, planes[0].mOffset);
         bytesUsed += planes[0].mOffset;
         // Workaround: filling length should not be needed. This is a bug of videobuf2 library.
-        buffer->setPlaneSize(0, bytesUsed);
+        // The length must not be below the device's sizeimage, which can exceed the frame's
+        // allocation size (e.g. a device that pads the frame size to whole pages), or the buffer
+        // is rejected. Use the device's size, as for multi-planar formats above.
+        buffer->setPlaneSize(
+                0, std::max(bytesUsed, mInputLayout->mPlanes[0].mSize + planes[0].mOffset));
         buffer->setPlaneBytesUsed(0, bytesUsed);
         // We only have one memory plane so we shall submit only one FD. The others are duplicates
         // of the first one anyway.
